√
5—
one unit of KCBS advantage above the classical floor.
The adjacency spectrum reinforces this picture: the
six golden-ratio eigenvalues{1 +φ, φ −1(×2),1−
φ−1,−φ(×2)}are the spectral fingerprint of graphs built
fromC 5 building blocks [24], providing spectral-graph-
theory evidence that the contextuality advantage is in-
herited from the KCBS subgraphs.
VI. EXPERIMENTAL FEASIBILITY
From a fundamental standpoint, KCBS is the simplest
qutrit witness, requiring only five events; this also makes
it attractive for experimental tests of foundational con-
textuality [6, 22]. On the other hand, contextuality can
have practical value and hence benefit from scaling. The
Quad-C5 graph addresses how contextuality can be orga-
nized and enhanced in a compact eight-event exclusivity
structure.
A qutrit implementation requires eight rank-one pro-
jectors
Πi =|v i⟩⟨vi|, i= 0,...,7,(18)
satisfying
ΠiΠj = 0 for every edge (i,j)∈E.(19)
The explicit construction in Appendix A provides such a
representation and therefore gives a direct experimental
prescription. The optimal input state is the eigenstate of
M=
X
i
Πi (20)
with largest eigenvalue. In the qutrit construction this
state yields
X
i
⟨Πi⟩= 1 +
√
5 (21)
(see Eq. (A4) in Appendix A).
The qutrit realization is non-injective: two pairs of
nonadjacent vertices are represented by identical projec-
tors (v 0 =v 6 andv 2 =v 3). Operationally, this corre-
sponds to repeated projector terms appearing in different
exclusivity contexts. This is consistent with the graph-
theoretic orthogonal-representation framework, but it
also shows why the qutrit advantage remains KCBS-like.
The larger contextuality gap of Quad-C5 is obtained only
in a four-dimensional realization, where the full Lov´ asz
value is reached numerically.
Possible platforms include spin-1 ion-trap and solid-
state systems [6, 25, 26], photonic path or orbital-
angular-momentum qutrits [21, 22, 27], NMR qutrits [2,
28], and four-level photonic or two-qubit systems [29].
In a four-dimensional implementation, Quad-C5 becomes
experimentally more appealing than the Wagner bench-
mark because it requires a strictly lower critical visibility
under depolarizing noise (v∗≈0.681 vs.v ∗(W)≈0.707).
VII. CONCLUSION
We have carried out an exhaustive search over all
11,117 connected non-isomorphic simple graphs on eight
vertices and identified Quad-C 5—an 8-vertex, 10-edge
graph containing four interlocking KCBS pentagons—as
the numerically certified unique maximizer of the contex-
tuality gap forn= 8. With ∆ = 0.46784, it surpasses
the Wagner graph (∆≈0.414) by∼13% while using two
fewer edges.
Unlike the Wagner graph, which numerical evidence
indicates requires a four-dimensional Hilbert space,
Quad-C5 is a confirmed qutrit witness: high-precision
numerics indicate thatη 3(Quad-C5) = 1 +
√
5, placing
this value in the same algebraic fieldQ(
√
5) as the KCBS
pentagon. In four dimensions, the full Lov´ asz bound is
saturated with orthogonality error below 10−13.
A depolarizing noise analysis reveals that Quad-C 5 at
d= 3 shares the critical visibilityv ∗ = 1/(3
√
5−5)≈
0.585 of the KCBS witness, an equality that follows an-
alytically from a uniform shift of the graph parameters
α,η 3, andn/d. Any qutrit apparatus sufficient for a
KCBS test can therefore certify Quad-C 5 contextuality
without relaxing noise requirements. Atd= 4, Quad-C 5
also strictly outperforms the Wagner graph in noise ro-
bustness, requiring 2.6% less visibility.
The present optimization is complementary to ear-
lier graph-based searches for fully contextual correla-
tions [12] and for graphs maximizing the relative mea-
sureϑ(G)/α(G) [13]. Those searches identify different
extremal structures. Here the objective is the absolute
CSW gap ∆(G) =ϑ(G)−α(G), for which Quad-C 5 is
the eight-vertex maximizer in our exhaustive search.
These results show that the optimal eight-vertex ex-
clusivity graph is already qutrit-contextual, while its full
Lov´ asz value gives a stronger four-dimensional contextu-
ality benchmark than the Wagner graph. They provide
concrete graph structures suitable for experimental tests
in three-level and four-level quantum systems. The find-
ing that then= 8 maximum requiresfeweredges than
the Wagner graph suggests that denser exclusion graphs
do not always yield stronger contextuality; the relation-
ship between graph sparsity and contextuality strength
warrants systematic future investigation.
